  "verbalization": "Neridienone A appears to have a consistent relationship with HepG2, a human liver cancer cell line, through its interaction with SNU-387. The evidence suggests that Neridienone A acts as a target for multiple compounds, including (1S,3R,6S,8R,11S,12S,15R,16R)-15-[(2R)-4-[(2R)-3,3-dimethyloxiran-2-yl]butan-2-yl]-7,7,12,16-tetramethylpentacyclo[9.7.0.01,3.03,8.012,16]octadecan-6-ol and (3R)-5,8,9-trimethoxy-2,2-dimethyl-3,4-dihydropyrano[2,3-b]quinolin-3-ol, both of which are targets of SNU-387. These compounds are implicated in the regulation of HepG2 cells, suggesting a potential mechanism of action involving SNU-387. The evidence is supported by a final score of 0.5434 for the first path and 0.5284 for the second and third paths, indicating a plausible but not definitive link between Neridienone A and HepG2. The mechanism likely involves the inhibition of SNU-387, which in turn affects HepG2 cells, though the exact molecular pathway remains to be elucidated.",
